WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:07.940
Hi, I'm Helen Thompson.

00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:09.820
I'm a producer here at Science News.

00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:12.220
This year for our year-end issue

00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:14.420
we decided to ask our beat writers

00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:16.340
what they're excited to cover next year.

00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:17.840
And here's what they said.

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:24.120
I'm Emily Conover

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:25.680
I'm the physics writer for Science News.

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:29.140
So the big discovery this past year was the detection

00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:31.420
of gravitational waves by the LIGO experiment.

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:33.760
And we expect to see more from them

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:34.560
in the coming year.

00:00:34.570 --> 00:00:35.750
So that's really exciting.

00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:36.990
Everyone is super pumped about

00:00:36.990 --> 00:00:38.070
gravitational waves right now.

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:39.420
Well particle physics is sort of

00:00:39.430 --> 00:00:40.900
in a weird state right now.

00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:42.420
It's a little bit of -- uh --

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:44.500
people are starting to get anxious.

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:46.420
We know that there's a lot of stuff

00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:48.040
that's still unexplained in particle physics.

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:49.340
Like we don't know what dark matter is --

00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:50.460
this stuff that makes up

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:52.540
a large portion of the matter in the universe.

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:54.640
So scientists are starting to look in some

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:57.380
more out-of-the-way corners

00:00:57.380 --> 00:00:59.220
for new physics.

00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:04.700
Hi, I'm Tina Hesman Saey

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:07.680
I'm the molecular biology reporter for Science News.

00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:11.440
CRISPR is a hot new molecular biology tool

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:12.680
that you may have heard of

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:16.540
because everybody is using it to edit genomes.

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:18.180
According to most scientists

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:20.860
CRISPR can do anything.

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:23.700
That's not exactly true,

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:25.700
but it can do a lot of stuff.

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:28.900
In 2017, you can expect, for instance,

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:31.600
to see clinical trials for cancer

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:34.500
starting out with CRISPR,

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:40.220
and also possibly bringing CRISPR back to its roots

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:45.300
to combat viruses like HIV and hepatitis.

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:49.540
I'm Thomas Sumner.

00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:51.000
I'm the earth and environmental sciences

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:52.360
for Science News magazine.

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:54.280
About 66 million years ago,

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:56.000
there was a devastating disaster on Earth.

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:58.460
And about three-quarters of all plant and animal

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:00.960
species got wiped out in a geologic instant.

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:04.720
And that's been a mystery for decades

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:05.940
You might have heard in school that

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:08.420
everything died from a big space rock impact.

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:10.040
But not everybody believes that.

00:02:10.120 --> 00:02:11.580
And so to help answer that question,

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:13.700
A team of researchers has gone back to the scene

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:15.380
of the crime, off the coast of Mexico,

00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:18.200
this giant 200-kilometer across crater,

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:19.120
called Chicxulub.

00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:21.940
They dropped three giant kind of legs into the sea,

00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:23.480
jacked themselves out of the water

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:24.820
so they had a stable platform,

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:26.540
and then drilled straight down,

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:28.480
into what's called the peak ring --

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:30.480
it's this ring of mountains within the crater itself.

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:32.760
So this drilling project and a lot of other research

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:34.760
projects regarding this horrible extinction event

00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:36.320
have already told us a lot.

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:38.580
But there's some big questions remaining

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:39.900
and a lot of the findings

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:41.400
are just making those questions even bigger.

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:44.160
So 2017 is going to be interesting one way or the other

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:46.020
whether or not we really solve

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:47.220
what killed the dinosaurs.

00:02:49.580 --> 00:02:50.960
Hi, I'm Meghan Rosen,

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:53.120
and I'm a staff reporter at Science News.

00:02:53.620 --> 00:02:56.560
So I cover everything from robots to dinosaurs

00:02:56.560 --> 00:02:58.400
But this year I spent a lot of time

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:00.920
focusing on the Zika virus outbreak.

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:02.620
So this year we've learned a lot about

00:03:02.620 --> 00:03:05.540
how Zika virus can affect a baby's brain and body,

00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:07.260
but we don't yet have a vaccine.

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:09.520
I think the earliest we can expect

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:13.060
to see a vaccine for Zika is in 2018 or 2019.

00:03:13.220 --> 00:03:15.280
Vaccines have already started working their way

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:16.800
through human safety trials,

00:03:16.820 --> 00:03:19.800
so I'm hoping that we see some results in 2017.

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:21.200
Two or three years might seem like

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:23.440
a long time to wait for Zika vaccine,

00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:25.780
but, in terms of vaccine development,

00:03:25.780 --> 00:03:27.220
it's a really short timeline.

00:03:28.940 --> 00:03:30.060
I'm Laura Sanders,

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:32.880
and I'm the neuroscience writer here at Science News.

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:36.780
One of the most interesting new technologies

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:39.560
that I've come across this year is call neural dust.

00:03:39.700 --> 00:03:42.560
It's these tiny crystals that scientists can

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:45.100
implant, so far just in animals, that

00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:48.820
can eavesdrop on nerve cell behavior, and ultimately

00:03:48.820 --> 00:03:51.880
they hope, can even control nerve cell behavior.

00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:55.200
Scientists are coming up with all of these different ways

00:03:55.220 --> 00:03:58.780
to really figure out what brain cells are doing

00:03:58.940 --> 00:04:00.660
and influence them in a way that

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:02.320
might actually help people

00:04:06.260 --> 00:04:08.160
Hi, I'm Christopher Crockett,

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:10.540
and I'm the Astronomy writer at Science News.

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:12.440
It's always a really cool year

00:04:12.440 --> 00:04:13.820
to be writing about Astronomy

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:15.760
We're always discovering new things,

00:04:15.820 --> 00:04:18.160
and I'm sure 2017 will be no different.

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:20.120
But there are a few things coming up

00:04:20.120 --> 00:04:21.320
that I'm really excited about.

00:04:21.620 --> 00:04:23.560
The Juno mission's at Jupiter,

00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:25.900
and it's been there since July.

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:27.520
But now it's going to really start

00:04:27.620 --> 00:04:28.840
doing its regular science,

00:04:28.840 --> 00:04:30.380
mapping the interior of Jupiter

00:04:30.380 --> 00:04:32.600
getting a 3-D picture of what the biggest

00:04:32.600 --> 00:04:33.760
planet in our solar system

00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:35.180
is like on the inside.

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:37.180
But i think one of the coolest things

00:04:37.180 --> 00:04:38.560
going on the solar system this year

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:41.300
is the end of the Cassini mission at Saturn.

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:44.380
After almost 20 years in space

00:04:44.380 --> 00:04:46.680
and over a decade at Saturn,

00:04:46.820 --> 00:04:48.500
the mission is  coming to an end.

00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:52.180
The spacecraft is going to dive into Saturn,

00:04:52.340 --> 00:04:53.800
plunge into its atmosphere.

00:04:53.980 --> 00:04:55.160
It's going to be making measurements

00:04:55.160 --> 00:04:57.740
and transmitting data as long as it can

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:00.080
until it's ... crushed to death.

00:05:00.540 --> 00:05:02.060
These are just some of the stories

00:05:02.060 --> 00:05:03.980
we'll be tracking in 2017.

00:05:03.980 --> 00:05:06.380
Stay tuned to see if we're right,

00:05:06.380 --> 00:05:08.900
and don't forget to subscribe to our channel.

00:05:09.140 --> 00:05:11.140
[music]

